\title{\textbf{The Forest: Physical Information Has a Path}\\[0.4em]
\large Worldtube Locality, Hagalaz Relations, and Threefold Closure in SAT/\HsH}
\author{Nathan McKnight\\\small Working draft with LLM-assisted formalization}
\date{21 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
This paper develops a conditional bridge among four ideas that have been emerging in SAT/\HsH: (i) the claim that information \emph{about an actual thing} requires a realized physical chain connecting that thing to a record; (ii) the possibility that what appears nonlocal in a three-dimensional spatial projection is local with respect to connectivity along a four-dimensional worldtube; (iii) Hagalaz as a common relation geometry for displacement, scale, frame rotation, and internal state; and (iv) a threefold circle/helix construction in which the historical SAT quantity $B=3/(4\pi)$ appears naturally as a candidate normalization of the $120^\circ$ tangent hierarchy. The first part is stated in ordinary information-theoretic language using mutual information and the data-processing inequality. The SAT/\HsH-specific parts are hypotheses and geometric constructions, not established physics. Their value here is to turn a loose conceptual cluster into a sequence of explicit definitions, conditional implications, and falsifiable mathematical tasks.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Status.} This is a working research note. ``Physical'' is used operationally to mean a realized, difference-bearing process or state in the world---including observations, photons, ink, voltages, neuronal activity, stored bits, spoken words, and any other actual carrier. No metaphysical claim about the ultimate substance of reality is required. SAT/\HsH mechanisms are treated as model assumptions to be tested, not as established physical facts.

\section{The hard premise: information about a thing has a path}

The starting claim is intentionally strong:

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Physical Information Principle.} If a record contains information \emph{about a particular actual referent}, then there exists a realized physical dependency path connecting that referent (or a physical trace generated by it) to the record.
\end{quote}

A photograph does not weaken this statement: the photograph is a physical state produced by light, chemistry or electronics. A sentence does not weaken it: the encoding convention is arbitrary, but the sound, ink, voltage, magnetic domain, optical state, or neural configuration that instantiates the sentence is physical. A thought does not weaken it: whatever else thought may ultimately be, in physics it is realized as a physical process capable of making physical differences.\mcite{PROV:NATHAN-20260921:PHYS-CHAIN}

The distinction is therefore not between ``physical information'' and ``symbolic information.'' It is between \emph{analog or structurally inherited encoding} and \emph{arbitrary or conventional encoding}. Both require realized carriers and transformations.

\subsection{A channel form}
Let $X$ denote a contingent state of a referent and $R$ a later record. A realized chain may be written schematically as
\begin{equation}
X \longrightarrow M_1 \longrightarrow M_2 \longrightarrow \cdots \longrightarrow M_k \longrightarrow R,
\label{eq:chain}
\end{equation}
where each $M_i$ is a physically realized mediator, transformation, measurement, memory, encoding, or transmission state. The arrows need not all be simple forward-in-time signals in a preferred coordinate system; they indicate physically realized dependency in whatever causal geometry the theory ultimately licenses.

If a segment of the chain is Markovian, the data-processing inequality gives
\begin{equation}
\MI(X;M_{i+1}) \leq \MI(X;M_i),
\label{eq:dpi}
\end{equation}
where $\MI$ is mutual information. Formal post-processing can preserve, reorganize, expose, compress, or discard information already present in its inputs; it cannot create mutual information with $X$ from inputs that contain none.\cite{CoverThomas}

For a mathematical inference $Y=f(R,K)$ using a record $R$ and background knowledge $K$,
\begin{equation}
\MI(X;Y) \leq \MI(X;R,K).
\label{eq:math_dpi}
\end{equation}
Thus mathematics can sharpen constraints enormously, but the referential content must enter through $R$, $K$, or both. The background knowledge is not exempt: its empirical content has its own physical ancestry. A bound such as ``larger than a breadbox, smaller than Italy'' may be mathematically propagated, but the facts that make the bracket applicable to this object were physically learned and physically indexed to it. Landauer's slogan that information is physical is related in spirit, although the present principle is stronger and specifically about referential ancestry.\cite{Landauer}

\subsection{The alter-the-thing test}
A useful operational test is:

\begin{quote}
Change the target property while holding fixed every physically available state on which the alleged knower actually depends. If the knower's record cannot change, then the record does not carry information about that changed property.
\end{quote}

This is not a claim that every correlation is a direct signal from target to observer. Common causes, inherited records, earlier observations, and extended physical constraints can all provide the path. The claim is that the correlation or reference cannot float free of a realized physical dependency structure.

\section{From a tree to the Forest}

For a given record $R$, define its \emph{information ancestry tree} $\Tree_R$ as the subgraph of realized states and channels on which the referential content of $R$ depends. Different records share mediators, common causes, laws encoded in apparatus, memories, and previous records. Their ancestry structures therefore overlap.

We call the union of these physically realized information-ancestry structures the \emph{Forest}:
\begin{equation}
\Forest = (V,E),
\end{equation}
with vertices $V$ representing physically realized states/events/extended sections and edges $E$ representing allowed difference-bearing relations or channels.

This formulation makes ``local'' a property of the edge relation, not merely a synonym for small Euclidean separation. Two vertices are local with respect to a physical theory when the theory places them in the primitive neighborhood relation through which influence or constraint is permitted.

