\section{SAT locality: projected distance versus worldtube adjacency}

SAT has historically taken a stronger position than merely saying that a four-dimensional block description is globally defined. A foundational SAT assumption is that an apparently nonlocal relation may be physically transmitted in a direction transverse to ordinary time propagation along an extended worldtube.\mcite{PROV:NATHAN-20260921:TRANSVERSE-NONLOCALITY}

Let $\sim_{3+1}$ denote ordinary local adjacency in the observed spacetime description and let $\sim_W$ denote native adjacency allowed by the SAT worldtube geometry. The proposal is not
\begin{equation}
\text{``there is no physical path,''}
\end{equation}
but rather
\begin{equation}
A \not\sim_{3+1} B
\qquad\text{while possibly}\qquad
A \sim_W B.
\label{eq:two_localities}
\end{equation}
In this language, apparent nonlocality is a mismatch between projected locality and native physical connectivity.

This is a substantive physical hypothesis, not a semantic escape from Bell's theorem. Bell-local hidden-variable models obey a factorization of the form
\begin{equation}
P(a,b\mid x,y,\lambda)
= P(a\mid x,\lambda)P(b\mid y,\lambda),
\label{eq:bellfactor}
\end{equation}
and quantum correlations violate the corresponding Bell inequalities.\cite{Bell,CHSH} If SAT is to reproduce those correlations by a real worldtube-transverse channel, then that channel must change the causal/factorization structure relative to ordinary $3+1$ locality; it cannot be merely an undisclosed ordinary local common cause. At the same time it must remain compatible with the observed inability to use entanglement alone for controllable superluminal signalling.

This gives the Forest a concrete task: specify the native graph $E_W$ well enough that information paths are physically explicit while the projected theory reproduces the correct operational constraints.

\section{Hagalaz as a relation geometry on Forest edges}

A current Hagalaz reconstruction represents a framed geometric object by
\begin{equation}
\mathcal S_i=(c_i,D_i,F_i;\mathcal P_i),
\end{equation}
where $c_i$ is a center, $D_i>0$ a declared scale, $F_i$ an orthonormal frame, and $\mathcal P_i$ internal state not reducible to frame orientation. A pairwise Hagalaz relation is then
\begin{equation}
\Hrel_{ij}=(\Delta c_{ij},\sigma_{ij},Q_{ij}),
\qquad
\Delta c_{ij}=c_j-c_i,
\qquad
\sigma_{ij}=\frac{D_j}{D_i},
\qquad
Q_{ij}=F_jF_i^{-1}.
\label{eq:hagalaz}
\end{equation}
This narrow representation has passed independent algebraic/type checks; it is not yet a Hagalaz dynamical law.\mcite{PROV:HAG-REP-20260920}\mcite{PROV:HAG-TEST-20260920}

For a closed three-node cycle,
\begin{align}
\Delta c_{12}+\Delta c_{23}+\Delta c_{31}&=0,\\
\sigma_{12}\sigma_{23}\sigma_{31}&=1,\\
Q_{31}Q_{23}Q_{12}&=I.
\label{eq:hagalaz_closure}
\end{align}
The point for the Forest is structural: a physical information edge need not be represented by a bare scalar distance. It can carry translation/displacement, relative scale, frame rotation, and additional internal plumbing. Hagalaz is therefore a candidate \emph{edge language} for comparing the geometric solvers that currently describe the same or adjacent structures in different coordinates.

For a longer closed path $\gamma=(1,2,\ldots,N,1)$, define the frame holonomy
\begin{equation}
Q_\gamma=Q_{N1}Q_{N-1,N}\cdots Q_{12}.
\label{eq:holonomy}
\end{equation}
Nontrivial $Q_\gamma$ is precisely the sort of local-to-global object capable of turning repeated local twist into a global closure condition.

\section{Three circles, the tangent hierarchy, and the appearance of $B$}

Consider $n$ equal circles of radius $r_f$ arranged cyclically around a central aperture so that adjacent bounding circles are tangent. Their centers form a regular $n$-gon. The central inscribed aperture radius is
\begin{equation}
h_n=r_f\left[\csc\!\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)-1\right].
\label{eq:aperture}
\end{equation}
For $n=2$, $h_2=0$: the central ``circle'' degenerates to the original two-circle kissing point. For $n=3$ the aperture first opens; $n=4$ gives the square case, and so on.

At the central kissing points, consistently oriented tangent directions advance by
\begin{equation}
\Delta_n=\frac{2\pi}{n}.
\label{eq:tangent_step}
\end{equation}
Thus
\begin{equation}
\Delta_2=\pi,\qquad
\Delta_3=\frac{2\pi}{3},\qquad
\Delta_4=\frac{\pi}{2},\qquad\ldots
\end{equation}
The historical SAT constant
\begin{equation}
B=\frac{3}{4\pi}
\label{eq:B_hist}
\end{equation}
is well attested in the project archive.\mcite{PROV:SAT26-MATH:B}

A simple exact identity now becomes visible. If one introduces the two-sided tangent normalization
\begin{equation}
B_n^{(\mathrm{tan})}\equiv\frac{1}{2\Delta_n},
\label{eq:Bn}
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
B_n^{(\mathrm{tan})}=\frac{n}{4\pi},
\qquad
B_3^{(\mathrm{tan})}=\frac{3}{4\pi}=B.
\label{eq:B3}
\end{equation}
The equality is exact. Its \emph{interpretation is not}. In particular, the factor of two in Eq.~\eqref{eq:Bn} must be geometrically justified rather than chosen because it produces $B$. Candidate meanings include the two orientations of a tangent line, a two-chirality normalization, or another paired tangent construction. Until one of those is forced by the geometry, Eq.~\eqref{eq:B3} is an exact candidate identification, not a derivation of the historical role of $B$.

